 agent serving by leveraging speculative tool execution. The design is motivated by two key observations about agent workloads. First, tool invocations exhibit strong application-level control flow (recurring tool-call sequences). For example, a <span id="S1.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">git clone</span> operation is almost always followed by <span id="S1.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">git checkout</span>. Second, tool parameters follow predictable data flow patterns, where arguments are implicitly derived from the outputs of earlier tools. As one example, the URL required by a <span id="S1.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">download</span> tool is typically extracted directly from the JSON output produced by a preceding <span id="S1.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">search</span> operation.</p> </div> <div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para"> <p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Operationalizing these insights, however, requires solving two fundamental problems: formalizing unstructured tool-call sequences and managing the risks of probabilistic execution. <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PASTE</span> addresses these through two components: a novel pattern abstraction that decouples control flow from data flow, and a risk-aware scheduler that preserves the execution performance of authoritative tools.</p> </div> <div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para"> <p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To resolve the formalization problem, <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PASTE</span> introduces the <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pattern Tuple</span> (context, prediction, function, probability). This abstracti
