CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: mechanism
SCALE_REALISM: L0 does not establish realistic workload behavior. The analytic/MC model is internally coherent, but the deciding parameters are unmeasured: real agent-step p_correct for tool name + leading args, actual GPU decode timing ratio t_tool/t_args, cancellation/side-effect cost w, and commit overhead. At r>=2 the packet’s own table requires p*=0.755-0.970 for w=1, so the claimed high-tool-latency regime only holds if real traces show very high correctness or very low wasted-work cost. PASTE reports real-trace/cluster-scale speculative tool execution results, so this claim needs a head-to-head against that framing, not only serialize-then-call.
RATIONALE: Prompt version v001. No exact shipping OSS implementation was found in vLLM/SGLang/LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM that starts an external tool as soon as partial tool-call JSON is decoded. However, PASTE is close prior work: it targets the same LLM-agent serial LLM-tool loop, speculates tool execution with predicted parameters, addresses side effects/resource contention, and reports 48.5% average task-completion reduction plus 1.8x tool throughput on a 32xA100 setup (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.18897v1). The packet’s proposed break-even p*=P/(S+P) is a standard speculative-execution tradeoff; the useful part is the agent-step instantiation and the negative finding that overlap gain saturates while miss cost grows with t_tool. Because the artifact is CPU-only simulation with no real p_correct or cancellation data, and because close prior work already evaluates speculative tool execution at realistic scale, this is candidate-grade only if the next pass measures real trace correctness and compares directly against PASTE-style pattern speculation and ordinary tool calling.
